s Are Less Likely:**\n- **(A) Spastic paralysis at the level of lesion** → Incorrect, because spasticity occurs *below* the lesion, not at the exact level.\n- **(B) Right-sided Horner’s syndrome** → Unlikely, as Horner’s syndrome (ipsilateral ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis) typically results from a lesion in the **sympathetic chain (T1–T2)**, not a C5 hemicord lesion.\n- **(C) Contralateral corticospinal tract involvement** → Incorrect, because corticospinal fibers cross in the medulla (not the spinal cord), so a hemicord lesion affects *ipsilateral* motor function.\n- **(E) Contralateral loss of vibration and proprioception** → Incorrect, because dorsal columns (vibration/proprioception) are *ipsilateral* to the lesion.\n\n### **Most Likely Finding: (D) Right-sided analgesia**\nThis matches the **contralateral spinothalamic tract involvement** in Brown-Séquard syndrome.\n\n### **Additional Considerations:**\n- The patient’s **left C5 hemicord lesion** would cause:\n  - **Left-sided weakness (ipsilateral corticospinal tract)** below C5.\n  - **Right-sided loss of pain/temperature (contralateral spinothalamic tract)** below C5.\n  - **Left-sided loss of vibration/proprioception (ipsilateral dorsal columns)** below C5.\n- **Horner’s syndrome is unlikely** unless the lesion extends to T1–T2.\n- **Spasticity develops over weeks**, not immediately, but the initial weakness is flaccid.\n\n### **Final Answer: (D) Right-sided analgesia**', tool_calls=Unset(), prefix=False, role='assistant'), UserMessage(content='Given the opinions from other medical agents, indicate whether you want to talk to any expert (yes/no). If not, provide your opinion.\n\nOpinions:\n(neurologist): Given the clinical scenario you’ve described, this patient’s presentation is highly suggestive of a **spinal cord injury (SCI) due to cervical manipulation**, likely resulting in a **Brown-Séquard syndrome (BSS)**—a classic syndrome caused by a hemisection of the spinal cord.\n\n### **Key Findings in Brown-Séq