f lesion (with progression below)**.)*\n\n#### **Why?**\n- **Spinal cord infarction (vascular hemicord lesion)** typically presents with:\n  - **Flaccid weakness** (immediate, due to **lower motor neuron involvement**).\n  - **Progression to spasticity** (weeks later, due to **upper motor neuron damage**).\n- **Option A** is the **best match** because:\n  - **Spasticity is expected below the lesion** (even if not "at" the level).\n  - **No other option aligns better** with the **expected neurophysiology** of a **vascular hemicord lesion**.\n\n#### **Why Options D and E Are Incorrect:**\n- **Option E (Contralateral vibration/proprioception)** is **anatomically wrong** unless there is **medullary involvement** (not suggested by the MRI).\n- **Option D (Contralateral analgesia)** is **wrong** because the **spinothalamic tract crosses immediately**—a left-sided lesion would **not** cause right-sided pain loss.\n\n---\n\n### **Revised Recommendations:**\n#### **1. Immediate Management:**\n- **Continue cervical immobilization** (to prevent further injury).\n- **Monitor for autonomic dysfunction** (e.g., **bladder/bowel changes, blood pressure instability**).\n- **Consider steroids** (if **inflammation is suspected**, though **vascular lesions are less responsive**).\n\n#### **2. Diagnostic Workup:**\n- **Repeat MRI with diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI)** to assess **vascular involvement** (e.g., **vertebral artery dissection, spinal artery occlusion**).\n- **CT angiography (CTA) of the neck** to rule out **vertebral artery dissection**.\n- **Electromyography (EMG) and nerve conduction studies (NCS)** to confirm **motor/sensory deficits**.\n\n#### **3. Consultation:**\n- **Vascular Neurologist** (to evaluate **vertebrobasilar insufficiency**).\n- **Neurosurgeon** (if **surgical intervention** is considered, e.g., **decompression or revascularization**).\n\n#### **4. Long-Term Prognosis:**\n- **Spinal cord infarction** often leads to **persistent deficits** (e.g., **weaknes