sychotic disorders.                                                                                                                       | **Incorrect**. Irrelevant to prognosis.                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| **(D) Male sex**          | **All Experts**: Male sex is **associated with a worse prognosis** in schizophrenia (earlier onset, more severe symptoms, higher relapse rates).                                                                         | **Incorrect**. This is a **negative prognostic factor**, not a predictor of good prognosis.                                                                                                                                     |\n| **(E) Negative symptoms** | **Psychiatrist & Clinical Psychologist**: Negative symptoms (e.g., flat affect) are **generally associated with a poorer prognosis**, though their **acute onset** in this case may be **secondary to the mood episode**. | **Incorrect**. While negative symptoms are present, their **acute nature** (rather than chronic) suggests they may **improve with treatment**, but they are **not as strong a predictor of good prognosis as affective symptoms**. |\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer: (A) Affective symptoms**\n**Why?**\n1. **Mood-Congruent Psychosis**:\n   - The patient’s **sudden behavioral change (grandiosity, social engagement, agitation)** is **highly suggestive of a mood-driven psychotic episode** (e.g., **schizoaffective disorder or bipolar disorder with psychotic features**).\n   - **Affective symptoms** (mania-like features) are **strongly associated with a better prognosis** than schizophrenia alone, as they respond well to **mood stabilizers + antipsychotics** and have a **lower risk of chronic negative symptoms**.\n\n2. **Treatment Implications**:\n   - **Mood stabilizers (lithium, valproate) + antipsychot