navailable) to assess **bony destruction**.\n   - **PET-CT** (if systemic recurrence is suspected).\n   - **Biopsy** (if imaging is indeterminate) to confirm **metastatic NSCLC**.\n   - **Serum markers** (e.g., **alkaline phosphatase, calcium**) to assess **bone turnover**.\n\n#### **Differential Diagnosis (Ruled Out):**\n| Option | Reason for Exclusion |\n|--------|----------------------|\n| **(A) Blastic lesions** | More common in prostate/breast cancer, not NSCLC. |\n| **(B) Bulging disc** | Would cause radicular pain, not dull night pain. |\n| **(D) Narrowing of disc space** | Degenerative, not neoplastic. |\n| **(E) Sacroiliitis** | Inflammatory, not consistent with cancer history. |\n\n#### **Conclusion:**\nThe **multidisciplinary team’s consensus** strongly supports **(C) Lytic lesions of the lumbar spine** as the **most likely imaging finding** in this patient. The **clinical presentation, cancer history, and expert reasoning** all align with **metastatic bone disease from NSCLC**.\n\n**Final Answer: (C) Lytic lesions of the lumbar spine** ✅'}

