👨‍⚖️  Moderator's final decision: {0.0: '### **Final Decision: Most Likely Test to Confirm the Diagnosis**\n\nAfter carefully reviewing the **multidisciplinary team discussion**, the **consensus among the orthopedic surgeon, neurologist, and physiatrist** strongly supports **Option (C) Straight Leg Raise (SLR)** as the **most appropriate test to confirm the diagnosis** in this patient.\n\n#### **Key Supporting Evidence:**\n1. **Clinical Presentation:**\n   - **Sudden onset of lower back/buttock pain radiating down the leg with an "electrical" sensation** after lifting a heavy object → **highly suggestive of lumbar radiculopathy (sciatica)**.\n   - **Obesity and diabetes** increase the risk of **degenerative disc disease and nerve compression**.\n\n2. **Diagnostic Test Rationale:**\n   - The **SLR test** is the **gold standard** for detecting **lumbar radiculopathy** (e.g., herniated disc compressing a nerve root).\n   - A **positive SLR** (pain radiating below the knee when lifting the leg) has **high sensitivity (91%) and moderate specificity (26%)** for sciatica due to disc herniation.\n   - Other tests (FABER, internal hip rotation, palpation) are **less specific** for nerve root compression and more relevant for **hip or sacroiliac joint pathology**.\n   - **Radiography (X-ray)** is **not definitive** for acute radiculopathy (MRI is superior for soft tissue evaluation).\n\n3. **Expert Agreement:**\n   - All three specialists (**orthopedic surgeon, neurologist, physiatrist**) independently concluded that **SLR is the best initial test** to confirm lumbar radiculopathy.\n   - No major disagreements were noted; the discussion converged on **SLR as the most reliable clinical maneuver** before considering advanced imaging (MRI).\n\n#### **Next Steps (If SLR is Positive):**\n- **MRI of the lumbar spine** (to confirm disc herniation, spinal stenosis, or other structural causes).\n- **Neurological exam** (reflexes, strength, sensation) to assess nerve root involvement.\n- **Consider conservative management** (physical therapy, NSAIDs, epidural steroid injections) or **surgical evaluation** if severe/progressive symptoms.\n\n### **Final Answer:**\n**(C) Straight Leg Raise (SLR)** is the **most likely test to help confirm the diagnosis** of lumbar radiculopathy (sciatica) in this patient.'}

