 not address the **underlying dopamine blockade or muscle rigidity**.\n   - **(A) Acetaminophen:** Only treats fever symptomatically but does not resolve rigidity or autonomic instability.\n   - **(D) Intubation:** Not yet indicated (patient maintains **O2 saturation 98% and adequate respirations**); this is a **last-resort measure** for airway protection if respiratory failure develops.\n\n5. **Consensus Among Experts:**\n   - All three specialists **independently concluded that dantrolene is the best next step**, reinforcing its appropriateness.\n   - The **emergency medicine physician and neurologist** emphasized NMS as the primary diagnosis, while the **toxicologist** considered synthetic cannabinoid toxicity but still recommended dantrolene for severe rigidity.\n\n#### **Additional Management Steps (Post-Dantrolene):**\n1. **Discontinue any dopamine antagonists** (if identified).\n2. **Continue aggressive cooling** (cooling blankets, ice packs, IV fluids).\n3. **Monitor for complications** (rhabdomyolysis, renal failure, electrolyte abnormalities).\n4. **Consider bromocriptine** (dopamine agonist) as adjunctive therapy if dantrolene is insufficient.\n5. **Prepare for intubation** if respiratory status deteriorates.\n\n#### **Conclusion:**\nGiven the **severity of symptoms, diagnostic likelihood of NMS, and expert consensus**, **dantrolene (B)** is the **definitive next step** in management. Delaying treatment could lead to **progressive organ failure or death**, making this a **time-sensitive intervention**.\n\n**Final Answer: (B) Dantrolene**'}

