[SystemMessage(content='You are a medical expert who conducts initial assessment and moderates the discussion.', role='system'), UserMessage(content='You are now a moderator in a multidisciplinary medical team discussion. Your job is to moderate the discussion, check whether the team has reached consensus, toward a correct and consistent final answer.', role='user'), AssistantMessage(content='Understood. As the moderator of this multidisciplinary medical team discussion, I will:\n\n1. **Facilitate the Discussion**: Ensure all relevant specialists (e.g., physicians, surgeons, radiologists, pathologists, nurses, pharmacists, etc.) have the opportunity to contribute their expertise.\n2. **Clarify and Summarize**: Periodically summarize key points to ensure clarity and alignment.\n3. **Check for Consensus**: Assess whether the team is moving toward a unified, evidence-based conclusion. If disagreements arise, I will guide the team to resolve them by referencing guidelines, literature, or further diagnostic steps.\n4. **Ensure Accuracy**: Verify that the discussion aligns with current medical standards, guidelines (e.g., NICE, WHO, specialty-specific protocols), and best practices.\n5. **Drive Toward a Final Answer**: Once consensus is reached, I will synthesize the team’s input into a clear, actionable plan (e.g., diagnosis, treatment strategy, or next steps).\n\n### Example Moderation Flow:\n- **Opening**: *"Today, we’re discussing [Patient X] with [presenting symptoms/diagnosis]. Let’s start with the history and exam findings. [Specialist A], could you summarize?"*\n- **Mid-Discussion**: *"We’ve heard from radiology and pathology. The imaging suggests [X], but the biopsy results are inconclusive. [Specialist B], how do we reconcile this?"*\n- **Consensus Check**: *"It seems we agree on [Y diagnosis/treatment], but [Specialist C] raised concerns about [Z]. Are there guidelines or trials to support either approach?"*\n- **Closing**: *"After reviewing the evidence, the team agrees on [final plan]. Does everyone concur, or are there outstanding questions?"*\n\n### Ground Rules:\n- **Evidence-Based**: Decisions must be supported by data, guidelines, or clinical experience.\n- **Respectful Debate**: Disagreements are welcome but must focus on the science, not individuals.\n- **Patient-Centered**: The final plan should prioritize the patient’s values, risks, and benefits.\n\nLet’s begin. **What is the case or topic for today’s discussion?** (Provide patient details, symptoms, test results, or a clinical dilemma.)', tool_calls=Unset(), prefix=False, role='assistant'), UserMessage(content='You are moderating the team. Decide whether the team has reached consensus on the final option.\nConsensus means the experts\' final answers point to the same option letter, or are clearly aligned.\n\nReturn EXACTLY one of the following (and nothing else):\nConsensus: YES\nConsensus: NO\n\nQuestion:\nA 74-year-old man undergoes an open cholecystectomy. Surgery is performed under general anesthesia for which the patient is intubated with an endotracheal tube. Directly following the procedure, his temperature is 37°C (98.6°F), pulse is 80/min, and blood pressure is 110/80 mm Hg. He is extubated as soon as he showed signs of alertness. Five minutes later, he becomes unresponsive. He has type 2 diabetes mellitus, coronary artery disease, and hypertension. He underwent stenting of the right coronary artery 10 years ago. He smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 40 years but quit 2 weeks ago for surgery. He drinks one to two beers daily. His current medications include metformin, enalapril, aspirin, atorvastatin, and a multivitamin. His temperature is 37°C (98.6°F), pulse is 102/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 130/80 mm Hg. Pulse oximetry shows an oxygen saturation of 73%. He is put on a non-rebreather mask with an FiO2 of 100%. Three minutes later, he is still unresponsive. His arterial blood gas analysis shows:\npH 7.32\nPCO2 33 mm Hg\