gest **6-month follow-up for syphilis in HIV+ individuals** to ensure treatment success.\n\n4. **Why Not Other Options?**\n   - **(A) LP first** → **Not necessary if treating empirically for neurosyphilis** (high risk in HIV+ with CD4 175).\n   - **(B) 3 doses + 3-month follow-up** → **Too early for secondary syphilis in HIV+ patients** (may miss treatment failure).\n   - **(D) Single dose + 6-month follow-up** → **Incorrect dosing for secondary syphilis**.\n   - **(E) Single dose + 6-month follow-up** → **Wrong regimen entirely**.\n\n#### **Final Management Plan:**\n✅ **Treat with 3 weekly doses of benzathine penicillin G (2.4 million units IM).**\n✅ **Start ART immediately** (to prevent further immune decline and syphilis progression).\n✅ **Follow up with RPR titers at 6 months** (and consider LP if neurologic symptoms develop).\n✅ **Monitor for treatment response** (serologic decline, clinical improvement).\n\nWould you like additional details on **ART initiation timing** or **long-term syphilis monitoring** in HIV+ patients?\n(neurologist): **No need to consult additional experts at this time.**\n\n### **Final Consensus & Management Plan:**\nAfter careful consideration of the **infectious disease specialist’s, neurologist’s, and public health/epidemiology expert’s opinions**, the **most appropriate next step is:**\n\n**✅ (C) Treat with three weekly injections of benzathine penicillin G (2.4 million units IM), followed by serologic titer follow-up in 6 months.**\n\n### **Why This is the Best Choice:**\n1. **HIV-Associated Syphilis & Neurosyphilis Risk:**\n   - The patient’s **CD4 count of 175** places him at **high risk for neurosyphilis**, even without neurological symptoms.\n   - **CDC guidelines recommend treating all syphilis infections in HIV-positive individuals with CD4 < 350 as if they have neurosyphilis** unless a lumbar puncture (LP) definitively rules it out.\n   - While the **neurologist initially suggested LP first (Option A)**, **treating empiricall