g:** **Tooth enamel erosion (Option C)**.\n3. **Secondary Findings:**\n   - **Amenorrhea** (hypothalamic-pituitary dysfunction from stress + weight fluctuations).\n   - **Normal BMI (21 kg/m²)** does not rule out bulimia (unlike anorexia nervosa).\n\n---\n\n### **Why Other Options Are Less Likely:**\n| **Option**               | **Rationale**                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n|--------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **(A) Malar rash**       | **Not related to bulimia.** Associated with **systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)** or **drug reactions** (e.g., hydroxychloroquine). No other systemic symptoms (e.g., arthralgias, photosensitivity) are present. |\n| **(B) Increased glucose** | **Unlikely.** Bulimia typically causes **hypoglycemia** (from starvation) or **normal glucose** (unless severe malnutrition). Hyperglycemia is not a primary feature. If present, it could indicate **insulin resistance** (rare in bulimia alone). |\n| **(D) Enlarged thyroid**  | **Possible but secondary.** While **subclinical hypothyroidism** is common in eating disorders (due to **nutritional deficiencies, stress, or hypothalamic dysfunction**), it is **not the most expected or urgent finding** in this acute presentation. The **dentist’s emphasis on oral health** and the **adolescent medicine specialist’s focus on behavioral patterns** take precedence. |\n| **(E) Elevated estrogen** | **Unlikely.** **Low estrogen** is expected in bulimia due to **hypothalamic amenorrhea**. Elevated estrogen would be atypical unless there is an **ovarian or exogenous source** (e.g., hormone therapy), which is not suggested here. |\n\n---\n\n### **Most Expected Additional Finding: (C) Erosion of Tooth Enamel**\n**Why This Is the Correct Answer:**\n1. **Direct Pathophysiology:**\n   - Chronic vomiting exposes teeth to **stomach acid (pH ~2.5)**, leading to **demineralization and erosion**.\n   - **Excessive toothbrushing** (a compensatory behavior in bulimia) further damages enamel.\n2. **Clinical Relevance:**\n   - **Objective and measurable** (visible on exam).\n   - **Actionable** (fluoride treatments, dental restorations, patient education).\n3. **Consistency with Other Findings:**\n   - **Russell’s sign** (calluses on hands) confirms purging behaviors.\n   - **Parotid enlargement** is another classic sign of bulimia.\n\n---\n\n### **Recommended Next Steps (Prioritized):**\n#### **1. Confirm Bulimia Nervosa (Today/This Week):**\n   - **Screening Tools:**\n     - **SCOFF questionnaire** (quick screen).\n     - **Eating Disorder Examination (EDE)** (gold standard).\n   - **Direct Questions:**\n     - *"Do you ever make yourself vomit after eating?"*\n     - *"Do you use laxatives, diuretics, or excessive exercise to control your weight?"*\n   - **Rule Out Other Causes of Amenorrhea:**\n     - **PCOS** (if hirsutism/acne present, though unlikely here).\n     - **Hyperprolactinemia** (if galactorrhea or visual field defects are present).\n     - **Cushing’s syndrome** (no classic signs like central obesity, striae, or hypertension).\n\n#### **2. Oral Health Intervention (Urgent):**\n   - **Dental Exam (Same Day or Within 1 Week):**\n     - Document **enamel erosion, caries, and gingival recession**.\n     - **Fluoride varnish application** (high-concentration) to protect remaining enamel.\n   - **Patient Education:**\n     - Avoid brushing immediately after vomiting (wait **30–60 minutes** to allow saliva to neutralize acid).\n     - Use a **soft-bristled toothbrush** and **fluoride toothpaste**.\n   - **Referral to a Dentist:**\n     - For **restorative care** (e.g., fillings, crowns) if erosion is severe.\n     - Consider **dental sealants** for high-risk areas.\n\n#### **3. Laboratory Work (Within 1–2 Weeks):**\n   - **Basic Metabolic Panel (BMP):**\n     - **Electrolytes (Na+, K+, Cl-)** to check for **hypokalemia or hypochloremic alkalosis** (from vomiting).\n   - **Thyroid Panel (TSH, free T4):**\n     - Rule out **subclinical hypothyroidism** (common in eating disorders but not urgent unless symptoms like fatigue or cold intolerance are present).\n   - **Complete Blood Count (CBC) and Iron Studies:**\n     - Check for **anemia** (common due to poor nutrition or gastrointestinal blood loss from vomiting).\n   - **Pregnancy Test (if not already done):** Already negative in this case, but good to confirm.\n\n#### **4. Psychiatric and Nutritional Referrals (Within 1 Week):**\n   - **Psychiatry:**\n     - **Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)** is the **gold standard** for bulimia.\n     - **Fluoxetine (60 mg/day)** if symptoms are severe (FDA-approved for bulimia).\n   - **Nutritionist:**\n     - **Gradual refeeding plan** to avoid **refeeding syndrome** (monitor **phosphorus, magnesium, potassium**).\n     - **Normalize eating patterns** (3 meals/day + snacks) to stabilize blood sugar and metabolism.\n\n#### **5. Monitor for Complications (Ongoing):**\n   - **Cardiac:**\n     - **ECG** to assess for **QT prolongation or arrhythmias** (from electrolyte imbalances).\n   - **Bone Health:**\n     - **DEXA scan** if there is a history of **low bone mineral density** or **long-standing amenorrhea**.\n   - **Gastrointestinal:**\n     - **Endoscopy** if **esophageal tears (Mallory-Weiss syndrome)** are suspected (e.g., hematemesis, severe retrosternal pain).\n\n#### **6. Family and School Support:**\n   - **Family-Based Therapy (FBT):**\n     - If the patient is **open to involving family**, this is **highly effective** for adolescents.\n   - **School Accommodations:**\n     - Reduce **academic stress** temporarily if it is a **trigger** (e.g., flexible deadlines, reduced course load).\n\n---\n\n### **How to Communicate with the Patient:**\nUse a **compassionate, non-judgmental, and collaborative approach**:\n1. **Validate Her Feelings:**\n   *"It’s clear that you’ve been struggling with stress and food, and that’s really hard. You’re not alone in this."*\n2. **Normalize the Discussion:**\n   *"Many teens go through phases where eating and emotions feel overwhelming. Let’s talk about how we can support you."*\n3. **Ask Open-Ended Questions:**\n   - *"Can you tell me more about what happens after you eat?"*\n   - *"Do you ever feel like you’ve lost control around food?"*\n   - *"Have you ever made yourself vomit or used laxatives?"*\n4. **Offer Hope and Next Steps:**\n   *"With the right support, this can get better. Would you be open to talking to a therapist or nutritionist? We can also connect you with a dentist to address your oral health."*\n5. **Avoid Judgment:**\n   *"I’m here to help, not judge. Let’s take this step by step together."*\n\n---\n\n### **Key Takeaway:**\nThis case **demonstrates the power of a multidisciplinary team** in managing **adolescent eating disorders**. The **most critical next steps** are:\n1. **Confirm bulimia nervosa** (screening + direct questions).\n2. **Address oral health urgently** (dental exam + fluoride treatment).\n3. **Connect her with psychiatric and nutritional support** (CBT, fluoxetine if needed, nutritionist).\n4. **Monitor for medical complications** (electrolytes, cardiac, bone health).\n\n**No further expert consultation is required** at this time, as the team’s consensus provides a **clear, actionable plan**.\n\n---\n### **Sample Script for Patient Referral:**\n*(For your records or to share with the patient:)*\n---\n**To:** [Psychiatry/Nutrition Clinic]\n**Re:** [Patient Name], 17-year-old female\n**Date:** [Today’s Date]\n\n**Patient Presentation:**\n[Patient] is a 17-year-old female who presents with **4 months of amenorrhea**, **binge eating**, and **stress-related triggers**. She denies sexu