ialist** all agree that:\n     - The resident is **at risk of exceeding duty hour limits** if he adds more hours.\n     - **Moonlighting should not be used to compensate for financial needs at the expense of patient safety.**\n     - The **safest and most compliant approach is to reduce hours** rather than risk fatigue-related errors.\n\n---\n\n### **Why the Other Options Are Incorrect:**\n| Option | Why It’s Wrong |\n|--------|---------------|\n| **(B) He is not allowed to work outside the scope of his residency training** | **Incorrect.** Moonlighting is **permitted** under ACGME rules, but **only if it does not exceed duty hour limits or interfere with training.** |\n| **(C) He may work longer shifts** | **Incorrect.** The ACGME **limits shifts to 16-24 hours max** (with mandatory rest periods). Longer shifts increase fatigue and error risk. |\n| **(D) He may work more hours on Sundays** | **Incorrect.** Sundays are **protected rest days** under ACGME rules and should not be used for moonlighting. |\n| **(E) He may work up to 20 more hours** | **Incorrect.** Adding **20 more hours** would push the resident to **~90 hours/week**, **violating the 80-hour limit** and increasing fatigue risk. |\n\n---\n\n### **Final Recommendations:**\n1. **The resident should reduce his moonlighting hours** to ensure compliance with the **80-hour weekly limit** and **fatigue mitigation policies**.\n2. **The residency program should monitor his schedule** to prevent duty hour violations and ensure patient safety.\n3. **Alternative financial solutions** (e.g., loan repayment programs, part-time non-clinical work) should be explored to avoid overcommitment.\n\n### **Conclusion:**\nThe **ACGME’s primary goal is patient safety**, and even if the resident is **technically within the 80-hour limit**, his current workload **risks fatigue-related errors**. The **most accurate and safest answer is (A): He is already overcommitting and must reduce the number of hours.**"}

