pically seen in MEN 2A.\n   - **Diffuse thyromegaly + elevated calcitonin** → **Medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC)**, a hallmark of MEN 2.\n   - **Bone pain + hypercalcemia** → Suggests **metastatic MTC** (more aggressive in MEN 2B) rather than primary hyperparathyroidism (HPT), which is more common in MEN 2A.\n   - **Fine tremors, anxiety, and diaphoresis** → Consistent with **catecholamine excess** (pheochromocytoma) and **autonomic hyperactivity** (common in MEN 2B).\n\n2. **Laboratory Findings:**\n   - **Elevated urinary VMA/metanephrines + serum epinephrine** → **Pheochromocytoma** (present in both MEN 2A and 2B, but more aggressive in 2B).\n   - **Elevated calcitonin** → **MTC** (defining feature of MEN 2).\n   - **Elevated PTH** → Could indicate **HPT**, but given the **bone pain and hypercalcemia**, it is more likely due to **MTC metastases or PTHrP secretion** (common in aggressive MTC).\n   - **Hyperglycemia** → Likely secondary to **catecholamine excess** or **stress response**.\n\n3. **Exclusion of Other Options:**\n   - **(A) Neurofibromatosis (NF1)** → No thyroid or adrenal tumors; presents with neurofibromas and café-au-lait spots.\n   - **(B) MEN 1** → Involves **parathyroid, pituitary, and pancreatic tumors**, but **no MTC or pheochromocytoma**.\n   - **(C) MEN 2A** → Includes **MTC, pheochromocytoma, and HPT**, but **lacks marfanoid habitus and aggressive bone pain**.\n   - **(E) Von Hippel-Lindau (VHL)** → Associated with **hemangioblastomas, renal cell carcinoma, and pheochromocytoma**, but **no MTC or HPT**.\n\n---\n\n### **Why MEN 2B Over MEN 2A?**\n| Feature               | MEN 2A                          | MEN 2B                          |\n|-----------------------|---------------------------------|---------------------------------|\n| **Marfanoid Habitus** | Rare                            | **Classic**                     |\n| **Bone Pain**         | Uncommon (unless HPT severe)    | **Common (MTC metastases)**     |\n| **HPT Prominence**    | **Core feature**                | **Less common/milder**          |\n| **MTC Aggressiveness**| Moderate                        | **High (early metastases)**     |\n| **RET Mutation**      | Codons 634/618/620              | **Codon 918 (or 883)**          |\n| **Autonomic Symptoms**| Less pronounced                 | **More severe (tremors, anxiety)** |\n\n**This patient’s profile aligns with MEN 2B due to:**\n- **Marfanoid habitus + bone pain** (pathognomonic for MEN 2B).\n- **Aggressive MTC + pheochromocytoma** (elevated calcitonin + catecholamines).\n- **Hypercalcemia likely due to metastatic MTC** (not primary HPT).\n\n---\n\n### **Next Steps (Based on Consensus)**\n1. **Genetic Testing:**\n   - **RET proto-oncogene sequencing** to confirm **codon 918 mutation** (MEN 2B).\n   - **Family screening** for at-risk relatives (prophylactic thyroidectomy may be indicated).\n\n2. **Imaging:**\n   - **Thyroid ultrasound + FNA** to confirm **bilateral MTC** and assess **lymph node involvement**.\n   - **Adrenal CT/MRI** to localize **pheochromocytoma**.\n\n3. **Management:**\n   - **Alpha-blockade (phenoxybenzamine)** for **10–14 days** before surgery (to manage pheochromocytoma).\n   - **Total thyroidectomy + central lymph node dissection** (urgent due to aggressive MTC in MEN 2B).\n   - **Adrenalectomy** (after alpha-blockade) for pheochromocytoma.\n   - **Monitor calcitonin levels** postoperatively (elevated levels suggest residual disease).\n\n4. **Hypercalcemia:**\n   - **IV fluids + bisphosphonates** if severe (avoid calcitonin if MTC is present).\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer:**\n**(D) Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN) 2B** is the **most likely diagnosis**, supported by:\n✅ **Marfanoid habitus + bone pain** (classic for MEN 2B).\n✅ **Aggressive MTC + pheochromocytoma** (elevated calcitonin + catecholamines).\n✅ **Hypercalcemia likely due to metastatic MTC** (not primary HPT).\n\n**No further expert consultation is needed at this stage.** Proceed with:\n1. **RET 