tical to confirm, but the **clinical picture strongly favors MEN 2B** due to its **aggressive phenotype**.\n\n---\n\n### **Why Not Other Options?**\n- **(C) MEN 2A**: Less likely due to **lack of marfanoid habitus** and **bone pain** (HPT is more common but less aggressive in MTC).\n- **(B) MEN 1**: Does not include MTC or pheochromocytoma.\n- **(A) Von Hippel-Lindau**: No thyroid or adrenal involvement.\n- **(E) Neurofibromatosis**: No thyroid or parathyroid tumors.\n\n---\n\n### **Action Plan**\n1. **RET Gene Testing**: Prioritize **codon 918** (MEN 2B) or **codons 634/618/620** (MEN 2A).\n2. **Thyroid Ultrasound + FNA**: Confirm MTC and assess lymph node involvement.\n3. **Adrenal CT/MRI**: Localize pheochromocytoma.\n4. **Alpha-blockade (phenoxybenzamine)**: Prepare for adrenalectomy.\n5. **Total Thyroidectomy + Central Lymph Node Dissection**: Urgent due to aggressive MTC in MEN 2B.\n\n---\n**Final Answer: (D) Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN) 2B** is the **most likely diagnosis** based on **clinical, biochemical, and expert consensus evidence**. Proceed with **RET testing and surgical management** as outlined.\n[oncologist] Error: Mistral failed.\n\n", role='user'), AssistantMessage(content='Consensus: YES', tool_calls=Unset(), prefix=False, role='assistant')]
